1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
A constraint is a predicate written once in the schema and re-checked on every write that touches the table.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:15,000
An INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE that would leave a constraint FALSE is refused; the statement is rolled back, not repaired.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Four families: keys, referential integrity, domain and CHECK constraints, and the general assertion.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:30,000
GATE asks what a constraint permits, not how to type it: which write is refused, and which rows disappear.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
A superkey is an attribute set whose values never repeat. On this three-row relation, five of the seven non-empty subsets are superkeys.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:45,000
A candidate key is a minimal superkey: drop an attribute and uniqueness is lost. There are two here, A and the pair (B, C).

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:53,000
The schema declares one candidate key as PRIMARY KEY; the rest are alternate keys, enforced with UNIQUE.

8
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:00,000
PRIMARY KEY = UNIQUE + NOT NULL. UNIQUE alone permits NULL, so it is weaker than a key.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Three constraints, one NULL, three verdicts: NOT NULL refuses it, UNIQUE accepts it, PRIMARY KEY refuses it.

10
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Against rows 1 and NULL, over the domain {NULL, 1, 2}: NOT NULL accepts 2 of 3, UNIQUE accepts 2 of 3, PRIMARY KEY accepts 1 of 3.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:23,000
UNIQUE accepts the second NULL because two nulls are not equal: the default is NULLS DISTINCT.

12
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:30,000
The SQL standard leaves this choice to the implementation; PostgreSQL 15 added UNIQUE NULLS NOT DISTINCT to make nulls collide.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:38,000
A CHECK is a Boolean expression over the row, and it is satisfied when the expression is TRUE — or UNKNOWN.

14
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:45,000
CHECK (a > 5) over NULL, 1 and 2: only the NULL row survives, because UNKNOWN is not FALSE.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:53,000
So CHECK (price > 0) does not stop a NULL price. NOT NULL is the constraint that does.

16
00:01:53,000 --> 00:02:00,000
CHECK (a > 5) and CHECK (NOT (a > 5)) both admit NULL, so the two together still do not exclude it.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:08,000
A foreign key names a column set in the child and a target in the parent; the target must be a primary key or a UNIQUE constraint.

18
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:15,000
MATCH SIMPLE, the default: if any referencing column is NULL, the row passes without a match at all.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:23,000
Over the nine candidates on {NULL, 1, 2} squared, with the parent holding (1,1): SIMPLE accepts 6, FULL accepts 2, PARTIAL accepts 4.

20
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:30,000
MATCH FULL demands all-or-nothing; MATCH PARTIAL is defined in the standard and not implemented in PostgreSQL.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:38,000
Five referential actions: NO ACTION, RESTRICT, CASCADE, SET NULL and SET DEFAULT. NO ACTION is the default.

22
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:45,000
NO ACTION may be deferred to the end of the transaction; RESTRICT may not, and it refuses even a delete whose end state would be legal.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:53,000
CASCADE deletes the children, SET NULL nulls the child key, SET DEFAULT writes the default and then demands that it exist in the parent.

24
00:02:53,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Only UNIQUE, PRIMARY KEY and FOREIGN KEY can be DEFERRABLE; CHECK and NOT NULL are always checked immediately.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:08,000
GATE CSE 2005: A is the primary key, C is a foreign key to A with ON DELETE CASCADE, and the tuple (2,4) is deleted.

26
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:15,000
Round 1: the key 2 has gone, so every row whose C equals 2 must go — (5,2) and (7,2).

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:23,000
Round 2: keys 5 and 7 have now gone. No row has C equal to 7, but (9,5) has C equal to 5, so it goes too.

28
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:30,000
Round 3: nothing has C equal to 9, so the closure is (5,2), (7,2) and (9,5) — three tuples, option C.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:38,000
GATE CSE 2017 Set 2: T1.P is the key, T1.Q references T2.R with cascade, and T2.S references T1.P with ON DELETE SET NULL.

30
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:45,000
Delete (3,8) from T1. T2 holds (8,3), whose S is 3, so SET NULL rewrites that row to (8, NULL).

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:53,000
No row of T2 is deleted, so the cascade from T1.Q to T2.R never fires and nothing comes back into T1.

32
00:03:53,000 --> 00:04:00,000
The number of additional T1 rows that must be deleted is 0, which is the official key.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:08,000
Which writes can break a foreign key? Insert into the child, or delete from the parent — those two, and not the other two.

34
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:15,000
Inserting into the parent only adds a key, and deleting from the child only removes a reference; neither can orphan anything.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:23,000
Updates split the same way: changing a child key to a missing value, or a parent key to a new value, can orphan rows.

36
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:30,000
GATE CSE 2004 Q13: with referential integrity holding, the child keys minus the parent keys is empty.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:38,000
SQL enforces the keys you declare, not the functional dependencies you can prove: a non-key dependency is not a constraint.

38
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Two tables that reference each other cannot be filled row by row, which is why deferral exists: check at COMMIT instead.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:53,000
Constraints beyond the five kinds need an assertion or a trigger; PostgreSQL has no CREATE ASSERTION, so a trigger is the route.

40
00:04:53,000 --> 00:05:00,000
A decomposition keeps its keys with UNIQUE and its links with foreign keys; losslessness is a property of the join, not a constraint.

